WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.616
Picture checking into a hotel for a long stay.

2
00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:11.195
The desk hands you a master key that opens every door, and a bill that grows every night.

3
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:17.607
On AWS, the master key is the root user, the email you signed up with.

4
00:00:17.607 --> 00:00:22.680
It goes in the safe, behind MFA, a second proof like your fingerprint.

5
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:27.385
For daily work you carry an ordinary key card instead.

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:30.980
Now the question almost nobody asks out loud.

7
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.341
My card is real, so why does AWS still say AccessDenied at some doors?

8
00:00:36.341 --> 00:00:37.260
Who decides?

9
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.616
Every command you type in the AWS CLI, AWS's command line tool, becomes a request.

10
00:00:44.616 --> 00:00:48.483
Picture it walking up to a door with a card reader.

11
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.592
The reader asks two questions.

12
00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:53.160
Is this card genuine, so who is calling?

13
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.471
And is this door on the card's list?

14
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:03.759
Inside the card are credentials: an access key ID, a secret, and for temporary credentials, a session token.

15
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:07.040
Notice: the card never hands its secret over.

16
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.733
The CLI uses it to sign the request, and only the signature travels.

17
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:16.014
AWS checks that signature with its own copy of the secret.

18
00:01:16.014 --> 00:01:18.688
They match: the first lamp goes green.

19
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:21.671
So where does your card come from?

20
00:01:21.671 --> 00:01:24.782
Many tutorials begin with: create an access key.

21
00:01:24.782 --> 00:01:26.079
Quick, and it works.

22
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.620
So, when does that key stop working?

23
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.903
Only when someone deactivates or deletes it.

24
00:01:35.903 --> 00:01:37.101
Never on its own.

25
00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:42.647
And it works for whoever holds it, copied from a laptop, a log file, or an old commit.

26
00:01:42.647 --> 00:01:49.717
That is a hotel card that still opens your room three years after checkout, for whoever found it in the car park.

27
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:51.309
So the point is this.

28
00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:56.664
A key that never expires is only as safe as every place it was ever copied to.

29
00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:01.936
Wouldn't it be nice if the card simply expired by itself?

30
00:02:01.936 --> 00:02:10.253
That is aws login, in CLI 2.32.0 or later: a day pass printed from your key card, through your browser.

31
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:17.169
It stores temporary credentials and refreshes them every 15 minutes, and no key ever lands on your disk.

32
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:19.415
Now the second lamp.

33
00:02:19.415 --> 00:02:23.269
The list of doors is an IAM policy, a JSON document.

34
00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:26.529
Here is the lab's reports reader, shortened.

35
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.516
Each rule has an Effect, Allow or Deny.

36
00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.657
An Action, the API call, like s3:GetObject.

37
00:02:32.657 --> 00:02:38.355
A Resource, what the call acts on, named by its ARN, its Amazon Resource Name.

38
00:02:38.355 --> 00:02:41.861
And an optional Condition that narrows the rule.

39
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.210
AWS decides every request the same way.

40
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.856
It starts from deny.

41
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.494
First request: read one report.

42
00:02:49.494 --> 00:02:50.862
A matching Allow?

43
00:02:50.862 --> 00:02:52.551
Yes, the second rule.

44
00:02:52.551 --> 00:02:54.643
Green, and the door opens.

45
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.479
Second request: delete that report.

46
00:02:57.479 --> 00:03:00.771
Nothing on the list allows s3:DeleteObject.

47
00:03:00.771 --> 00:03:02.991
No Allow, so it stays denied.

48
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.286
Third request: list everything in the bucket.

49
00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.898
Look at the first rule.

50
00:03:07.898 --> 00:03:11.051
It allows s3:ListBucket, on this very bucket.

51
00:03:11.051 --> 00:03:12.312
Allowed or denied?

52
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:16.878
It is tempting to say allowed.

53
00:03:16.878 --> 00:03:19.405
The action matches, the bucket matches.

54
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.213
But the Condition says the prefix must start with reports.

55
00:03:23.213 --> 00:03:26.141
Listing the whole bucket sends an empty prefix.

56
00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:27.449
Empty does not match.

57
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.346
So no Allow matches, and we fall back to the default.

58
00:03:31.346 --> 00:03:31.841
Denied.

59
00:03:31.841 --> 00:03:32.335
Well...

60
00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:36.011
that is a locked door we were sure we had a key for.

61
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.161
Ask for the reports folder instead, and the prefix matches.

62
00:03:40.161 --> 00:03:41.504
The lamp goes green.

63
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:43.009
Last rule.

64
00:03:43.009 --> 00:03:48.424
Say another policy on the same caller holds one Deny for reading reports.

65
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.323
Now the read meets an Allow and a Deny.

66
00:03:51.323 --> 00:03:54.396
An explicit Deny anywhere beats every Allow.

67
00:03:54.396 --> 00:03:54.885
Denied.

68
00:03:54.885 --> 00:03:59.144
The manager's veto: it doesn't matter how many cards say yes.

69
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:01.523
So, the whole rule.

70
00:04:01.523 --> 00:04:02.694
Start at deny.

71
00:04:02.694 --> 00:04:03.949
An Allow opens.

72
00:04:03.949 --> 00:04:06.040
A Deny beats every Allow.

73
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.612
Programs, like a server, should not carry a stored key at all.

74
00:04:10.612 --> 00:04:11.780
They assume a role.

75
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.161
Picture a contractor's badge waiting at the front desk.

76
00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:17.139
A role carries two policies.

77
00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:22.621
The permissions policy, like the list we just read, says which doors the badge opens.

78
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.348
The trust policy says who may collect the badge at the desk.

79
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:30.169
In the lab's trust policy the Principal ends in root.

80
00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:36.205
That means the whole account: any identity in it whose own policy allows sts:AssumeRole.

81
00:04:36.205 --> 00:04:39.567
It does not mean the root user, despite the name.

82
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:48.529
aws sts assume-role checks both sides: your own policy must allow it, and the trust policy must let you collect it.

83
00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:53.508
Then it hands back temporary credentials that last an hour by default.

84
00:04:53.508 --> 00:04:55.583
Their key ID starts with ASIA.

85
00:04:55.583 --> 00:05:00.356
Long-lived keys start with AKIA, so you can tell them apart in a log.

86
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:02.177
Two guardrails.

87
00:05:02.177 --> 00:05:04.335
Check a policy before it bites.

88
00:05:04.335 --> 00:05:10.946
IAM Access Analyzer validates it, and the policy simulator answers: would this call be allowed?

89
00:05:10.946 --> 00:05:12.616
without making the call.

90
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.027
And the practice emulator, moto, allows every call.

91
00:05:16.027 --> 00:05:22.797
A hotel with cardboard doors: good for learning the corridors, useless for learning which doors are locked.

92
00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:25.189
So the moral of the story.

93
00:05:25.189 --> 00:05:29.011
The card proves who you are without ever sending its secret.

94
00:05:29.011 --> 00:05:33.279
Every door starts locked, an Allow opens it, and any Deny shuts it.

95
00:05:33.279 --> 00:05:35.827
And the best cards expire by themselves.

96
00:05:36.533 --> 00:05:38.462
A quick check before you go.

97
00:05:38.462 --> 00:05:45.006
The trust policy lets you collect the badge, but your own policy does not allow sts:AssumeRole.

98
00:05:45.006 --> 00:05:46.452
Do you get the badge?

99
00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:49.301
No.

100
00:05:49.301 --> 00:05:53.432
assume-role checks both sides, and your side said no.
